WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.221 --> 00:00:02.504
- Even today as big as Nike is

00:00:02.504 --> 00:00:04.058
with over 60,000 employees,

00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:05.366
the key thing is people.

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:06.964
I think in many ways

00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:08.522
the success that Nike has today

00:00:08.522 --> 00:00:10.119
really came out of the soul searching

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:11.429
that was done in the mid 1980s

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:12.733
when Reebok went past us.

00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:14.303
I know they asked me

00:00:14.303 --> 00:00:15.738
on one of the rare TV interviews

00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:16.678
from 25 years ago

00:00:16.678 --> 00:00:18.355
if I wanted to meet
the president of Reebok

00:00:18.355 --> 00:00:19.188
and I said no.

00:00:19.188 --> 00:00:20.563
I says I don't know him,

00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:21.423
I don't like him,

00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:22.653
and I don't want to like him.

00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:24.696
It's more than just a business.

00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:27.355
It's more than just sports.

00:00:27.355 --> 00:00:28.745
It is a mission.

00:00:28.745 --> 00:00:31.495
- He's an American business
magnet and philanthropist.

00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:32.561
He's the Co-Founder

00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:34.363
and Chairman Emeritus of Nike.

00:00:34.363 --> 00:00:38.373
He has an estimated net worth
of 25.5 billion dollars.

00:00:38.373 --> 00:00:40.116
He's Phil Knight and here's my take

00:00:40.116 --> 00:00:41.887
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:43.423
Rule number eight is my personal favorite

00:00:43.423 --> 00:00:45.545
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.859
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:48.250
Also, as Phil is talking,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.925
if he says something
that is meaningful to you

00:00:49.925 --> 00:00:51.138
that you want to remember,

00:00:51.138 --> 00:00:53.272
make sure to write it
down in the comments below

00:00:53.272 --> 00:00:54.580
and put quotes around it

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.913
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.168
- You're a runner

00:01:01.168 --> 00:01:03.978
with a track coach that
you're very fond of,

00:01:03.978 --> 00:01:05.082
an accountant,

00:01:05.082 --> 00:01:06.993
and a Stanford Business School grad.

00:01:06.993 --> 00:01:07.826
- All those things.

00:01:07.826 --> 00:01:09.071
- And all of that produced a crazy idea.

00:01:09.071 --> 00:01:10.503
How did it come to you?

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:11.842
- Well it was a combination of two things.

00:01:11.842 --> 00:01:13.378
It came off the running track at Oregon

00:01:13.378 --> 00:01:14.848
and out of the classroom

00:01:14.848 --> 00:01:17.536
at Stanford Business School.

00:01:17.536 --> 00:01:20.270
And that Bowerman was
always tinkering with shoes,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.508
and he began to have
some of his good runners,

00:01:23.508 --> 00:01:25.791
not just his hacks like me,

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:26.819
wear the shoes.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.203
And they were wearing
them quite successfully,

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:30.539
and he was very sincere in believing

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:31.984
the weight of the shoe was

00:01:31.984 --> 00:01:33.664
very important to a runner.

00:01:33.664 --> 00:01:36.203
And so that registered.

00:01:36.203 --> 00:01:38.750
All the best shoes were
being made in Germany.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.282
It made no sense that
you would make shoes,

00:01:40.282 --> 00:01:41.464
Adidas and Puma,

00:01:41.464 --> 00:01:43.482
make no sense that they'd
be made in Germany.

00:01:43.482 --> 00:01:44.934
So I wrote the paper about

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:46.399
they should make them in Japan

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.299
and the idea built and built and built,

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:50.985
and it became a crazy idea to everybody

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:52.664
but me and my college professor,

00:01:52.664 --> 00:01:54.223
and then my old track coach.

00:01:54.223 --> 00:01:57.874
- If you were talking to a
young entrepreneur today,

00:01:57.874 --> 00:02:00.369
what would you say about

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:02.783
how a great team gets put together?

00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:05.145
About the role of a CEO

00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:07.576
in supporting, shaping,

00:02:07.576 --> 00:02:09.167
interacting with that team?

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.549
- Yeah, it's life and death.

00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:12.181
I think even today

00:02:12.181 --> 00:02:14.879
as big as Nike is with
over 60,000 employees,

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:16.927
the key thing is people.

00:02:16.927 --> 00:02:18.072
So when you're starting out

00:02:18.072 --> 00:02:20.112
obviously getting the right people,

00:02:20.112 --> 00:02:21.622
that's life and death.

00:02:21.622 --> 00:02:23.583
And then not only the right people

00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:25.383
but getting them to work together

00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:26.816
and work for a common purpose.

00:02:26.816 --> 00:02:28.001
I've always said,

00:02:28.001 --> 00:02:29.351
two nines working together

00:02:29.351 --> 00:02:31.853
will beat two tens working apart any day.

00:02:31.853 --> 00:02:32.797
- And how do you do that?

00:02:32.797 --> 00:02:34.883
- Well, get the right
people is number one,

00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:37.010
and then giving them the environment

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.013
and maybe a little bit of leadership

00:02:39.013 --> 00:02:40.756
that allows them to keep doing that.

00:02:40.756 --> 00:02:43.661
- And the environment for good,

00:02:43.661 --> 00:02:45.036
for the ability to get

00:02:45.036 --> 00:02:46.436
different people to work together.

00:02:46.436 --> 00:02:49.498
I'm looking for how you would articulate,

00:02:49.498 --> 00:02:52.940
what is that thing you
put in the environment?

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.781
- Well, now you're
getting into management,

00:02:54.781 --> 00:02:56.047
and there's all kinds of

00:02:56.047 --> 00:02:58.380
different management styles.

00:03:00.232 --> 00:03:01.439
And you have to kind of recognize

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:04.413
that each person is an individual.

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:06.236
But I always liked Sparky Anderson,

00:03:06.236 --> 00:03:07.707
the manager of the Cincinnati Reds.

00:03:07.707 --> 00:03:09.839
He says, do you treat
all players the same?

00:03:09.839 --> 00:03:11.227
Hell no, he says.

00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:12.455
If Johnny Bench wants a day off,

00:03:12.455 --> 00:03:13.522
he gets it off.

00:03:13.522 --> 00:03:14.497
If a rookie wants a day off,

00:03:14.497 --> 00:03:15.822
I said, get your ass out on the field.

00:03:15.822 --> 00:03:18.613
And I think that's a good
management principle by itself.

00:03:18.613 --> 00:03:20.493
The key thing for us was the product.

00:03:20.493 --> 00:03:22.026
And our whole focus

00:03:22.026 --> 00:03:24.150
for the first 10 years of the company

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.620
was building a better mousetrap,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.682
having a better running shoe,

00:03:26.682 --> 00:03:27.909
having a better basketball shoe,

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:30.320
having more cushioning in different shoes.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.368
And innovating every six months

00:03:32.368 --> 00:03:34.213
and spending a lot of money
on research and development.

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:36.236
Spending a lot of money on a lab.

00:03:36.236 --> 00:03:38.164
And the product process

00:03:38.164 --> 00:03:40.415
really was developed far in advance

00:03:40.415 --> 00:03:42.294
of anything else that we did.

00:03:42.294 --> 00:03:44.894
The most important thing is you have to...

00:03:44.894 --> 00:03:45.753
there's two sides.

00:03:45.753 --> 00:03:46.962
There's really sort of the intellectual

00:03:46.962 --> 00:03:48.038
or mental side.

00:03:48.038 --> 00:03:51.271
You have to pick a
business that has a niche.

00:03:51.271 --> 00:03:53.112
You have to have a reason to succeed.

00:03:53.112 --> 00:03:54.517
You have to be something
like we were doing

00:03:54.517 --> 00:03:56.356
and say Japan can beat Germany

00:03:56.356 --> 00:03:58.439
in the manufacturing of
this type of a product.

00:03:58.439 --> 00:03:59.850
That was the niche really.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:00.712
And then on that,

00:04:00.712 --> 00:04:01.545
you just have to have

00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:03.184
a total and complete passion,

00:04:03.184 --> 00:04:07.753
because there's going to
be so many dark moments.

00:04:07.753 --> 00:04:08.970
I think the dark moments are true

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.213
for any entrepreneur

00:04:11.213 --> 00:04:12.972
or any startup company.

00:04:12.972 --> 00:04:15.098
I'm sure that you talk
to Steve Jobs in Apple,

00:04:15.098 --> 00:04:17.671
you'd see many, many dark, dark moments.

00:04:17.671 --> 00:04:20.003
Ours happened to last a little
longer than most of 'em,

00:04:20.003 --> 00:04:22.286
but you really emotionally
have to be prepared

00:04:22.286 --> 00:04:24.083
for those dark moments.

00:04:24.083 --> 00:04:27.662
- I think every entrepreneur
I've ever spoken to

00:04:27.662 --> 00:04:29.155
would echo that.

00:04:29.155 --> 00:04:30.364
That there are dark moments

00:04:30.364 --> 00:04:32.000
when everything's on the line.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.552
- That's the life of an entrepreneur.

00:04:33.552 --> 00:04:34.697
And so you're going to be into it,

00:04:34.697 --> 00:04:36.478
you better be prepared for that.

00:04:36.478 --> 00:04:37.745
Before there was success,

00:04:37.745 --> 00:04:39.096
there was defeat.

00:04:39.096 --> 00:04:41.224
We were kicked out of two banks.

00:04:41.224 --> 00:04:43.481
We borrowed money once
from a shoebox manufacturer

00:04:43.481 --> 00:04:44.873
to meet the payroll.

00:04:44.873 --> 00:04:45.706
On another time,

00:04:45.706 --> 00:04:48.544
we had to sell $2500 at
a National PE Convention

00:04:48.544 --> 00:04:50.626
in Seattle, Washington, on a Friday

00:04:50.626 --> 00:04:53.081
so we could meet payroll on Monday.

00:04:53.081 --> 00:04:54.530
In all those cases,

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.411
the lessons from the
fields and the courts,

00:04:56.411 --> 00:04:58.661
the lessons of competitive response

00:04:58.661 --> 00:05:00.619
were the most valuable of all.

00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:01.967
I think in many ways,

00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:03.601
the success that Nike has today

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:05.289
really came out of the soul searching

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:06.518
that was done in the mid 1980s

00:05:06.518 --> 00:05:07.773
when Reebok went past us,

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.530
that essentially we had to decide

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.022
who we were and what we were

00:05:12.022 --> 00:05:13.724
and try and build off of that

00:05:13.724 --> 00:05:16.490
and really defend those values

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.532
that really determined,

00:05:18.532 --> 00:05:22.907
that dictated what our
persona was as a company.

00:05:22.907 --> 00:05:25.487
- [Narrator] Knight refocused
the brand on pure sport

00:05:25.487 --> 00:05:27.123
and reorganized the company.

00:05:27.123 --> 00:05:28.924
Ten Commandments emerged.

00:05:28.924 --> 00:05:30.967
Break the rules, fight the law.

00:05:30.967 --> 00:05:33.751
This is as much about
battle as out business.

00:05:33.751 --> 00:05:35.426
It won't be pretty.

00:05:35.426 --> 00:05:37.593
And lastly, if we do the right things,

00:05:37.593 --> 00:05:40.207
we'll make money damn near automatic.

00:05:40.207 --> 00:05:41.554
- I have in my travels

00:05:41.554 --> 00:05:42.822
occasionally met promising young people

00:05:42.822 --> 00:05:43.923
who insist they are not

00:05:43.923 --> 00:05:46.458
going to ask for help along the way.

00:05:46.458 --> 00:05:49.934
They want to figure it out themselves.

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:51.732
Mine was the opposite approach.

00:05:51.732 --> 00:05:53.478
It is hard enough out there.

00:05:53.478 --> 00:05:55.315
Get all the help you can.

00:05:55.315 --> 00:05:57.323
Getting help really is just a part

00:05:57.323 --> 00:06:00.101
of a lifelong search for wisdom.

00:06:00.101 --> 00:06:03.057
Brands are one of the most
interesting parts of business.

00:06:03.057 --> 00:06:03.916
But I mean basically,

00:06:03.916 --> 00:06:05.676
a brand stands for something.

00:06:05.676 --> 00:06:08.333
It creates an emotion in a person's mind

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:09.992
to either positive or negative,

00:06:09.992 --> 00:06:11.979
that brings out all kinds of reactions

00:06:11.979 --> 00:06:13.777
to that brand.

00:06:13.777 --> 00:06:15.934
And when we got to start our own brand

00:06:15.934 --> 00:06:17.243
and have our own name,

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:19.119
we were very ambitious
in some of the things

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:21.039
that we wanted it to stand for.

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:22.346
But as you stop and think about

00:06:22.346 --> 00:06:23.956
brands around the world,

00:06:23.956 --> 00:06:26.860
from Sony and Coca Cola and Rolls Royce.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.592
Where they all stand for
very different things,

00:06:28.592 --> 00:06:31.116
but they all create an emotional response

00:06:31.116 --> 00:06:33.998
and something that's
really strong and powerful.

00:06:33.998 --> 00:06:34.831
- I was developing

00:06:34.831 --> 00:06:37.169
an unhealthy contempt for Adidas, you say,

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:39.131
or maybe it was healthy.

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:40.682
I despised them.

00:06:40.682 --> 00:06:41.515
- Yeah.

00:06:41.515 --> 00:06:43.466
- I'm curious what you make
of the competition right now?

00:06:43.466 --> 00:06:45.960
Adidas is coming back from a soft patch.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.286
And there are upstarts like Under Armour

00:06:47.286 --> 00:06:48.781
that are making headway

00:06:48.781 --> 00:06:51.261
on athletes and on sales.

00:06:51.261 --> 00:06:54.821
- Well I think, I'm sort
of a worrier by nature

00:06:54.821 --> 00:06:57.150
and so everybody is concerned.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.080
But I'm still the same as I was before.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.158
I know they asked me

00:07:01.158 --> 00:07:03.531
on one of the rare TV
interviews from 25 years ago

00:07:03.531 --> 00:07:05.163
if I wanted to meet the
president of Reebok,

00:07:05.163 --> 00:07:06.458
and I said no.

00:07:06.458 --> 00:07:07.643
I says I don't know him,

00:07:07.643 --> 00:07:08.476
I don't like him

00:07:08.476 --> 00:07:10.026
and I don't want to like him.

00:07:10.026 --> 00:07:12.192
And I still feel that way
about the competition.

00:07:12.192 --> 00:07:14.207
- What about Under Armour
in the U.S. market?

00:07:14.207 --> 00:07:15.586
- They're one of 'em.
- Is there room for three?

00:07:15.586 --> 00:07:16.419
- They're one of 'em.

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:18.771
There are lots of them
that we compete with.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.431
But believe me, we will compete.

00:07:21.431 --> 00:07:23.682
- [Narrator] Phil Knight built
Nike's corporate headquarters

00:07:23.682 --> 00:07:25.192
in Portland, Oregon,

00:07:25.192 --> 00:07:28.109
as a shrine to sporting excellence.

00:07:29.128 --> 00:07:30.625
It's part cathedral,

00:07:30.625 --> 00:07:32.585
part university campus.

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:35.856
Each building has its own patron saint.

00:07:35.856 --> 00:07:37.773
Icons adorn every wall.

00:07:40.716 --> 00:07:42.348
As they walk down the cloisters,

00:07:42.348 --> 00:07:44.428
disciples are encouraged to contemplate

00:07:44.428 --> 00:07:46.065
the deeds of Nike heroes

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:47.482
past and present.

00:07:50.237 --> 00:07:53.469
- Heritage is the glue that holds

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:54.552
great companies together.

00:07:54.552 --> 00:07:56.430
Because you have to
love what you're doing.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.088
Because this is the way people have chosen

00:07:58.088 --> 00:07:59.683
to work in the world.

00:07:59.683 --> 00:08:01.604
And if you're going to work for a company,

00:08:01.604 --> 00:08:03.466
you might as well love what you do.

00:08:03.466 --> 00:08:04.299
And for us,

00:08:04.299 --> 00:08:07.034
it's understanding the
roots that gives people

00:08:07.034 --> 00:08:08.547
the energy and the fire

00:08:08.547 --> 00:08:11.273
and the appreciation for
competition in sport.

00:08:11.273 --> 00:08:12.708
And that passion,

00:08:12.708 --> 00:08:13.981
you can only get it if

00:08:13.981 --> 00:08:16.029
you're hooked into why you exist.

00:08:16.029 --> 00:08:17.203
Our employees understand,

00:08:17.203 --> 00:08:18.103
what are we doing here?

00:08:18.103 --> 00:08:19.656
Why do we exist?

00:08:19.656 --> 00:08:23.943
And if they understand
that, everybody excels.

00:08:23.943 --> 00:08:25.454
- [Narrator] To approach the main door,

00:08:25.454 --> 00:08:27.864
workers walk along the Rocky Road,

00:08:27.864 --> 00:08:30.723
which symbolizes man's
journey from an uncertain past

00:08:30.723 --> 00:08:33.460
to a more promising Nike future.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.663
- It's more than just a business.

00:08:35.663 --> 00:08:38.276
It's more than just sports.

00:08:38.276 --> 00:08:39.958
It is a mission.

00:08:39.958 --> 00:08:42.219
This is what we've committed
our lives to doing,

00:08:42.219 --> 00:08:45.386
so let's make it something worthwhile.

00:08:47.452 --> 00:08:48.508
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:48.508 --> 00:08:50.016
I made this video because

00:08:50.016 --> 00:08:53.123
MrOldFashionedlover asked me to.

00:08:53.123 --> 00:08:54.263
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:08:54.263 --> 00:08:56.027
that you want me to profile next,

00:08:56.027 --> 00:08:57.499
leave it down in the comments below,

00:08:57.499 --> 00:08:58.806
and I'll see what I could do.

00:08:58.806 --> 00:08:59.639
I'd just love to know

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:00.472
which of Phil's top 10 rules

00:09:00.472 --> 00:09:02.436
had the biggest impact on you and why?

00:09:02.436 --> 00:09:04.031
What change you're going to
make in your life or business

00:09:04.031 --> 00:09:06.028
as a result of watching this video?

00:09:06.028 --> 00:09:06.861
Leave in the comments,

00:09:06.861 --> 00:09:08.400
I will join in the discussion.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.391
Finally, I want to take a quick shout out

00:09:09.391 --> 00:09:10.625
to Orquidea Gutierez.

00:09:10.625 --> 00:09:12.176
I hope I pronounced that okay.

00:09:12.176 --> 00:09:13.850
Thank you so much for buying my book.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:15.116
It really means a lot to me.

00:09:15.116 --> 00:09:15.949
For those of you watching,

00:09:15.949 --> 00:09:17.572
you want a chance at a
shout out in a future video,

00:09:17.572 --> 00:09:19.786
make sure to pick up a copy of the book

00:09:19.786 --> 00:09:20.707
and email in your receipt

00:09:20.707 --> 00:09:21.812
so we can keep track.

00:09:21.812 --> 00:09:23.164
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:23.164 --> 00:09:24.635
Continue to believe

00:09:24.635 --> 00:09:26.472
or whatever your one word is,

00:09:26.472 --> 00:09:28.305
and I'll see you soon.

00:09:33.917 --> 00:09:37.446
- The swoosh, the very famous Nike swoosh.

00:09:37.446 --> 00:09:39.201
How much you pay for that design?

00:09:39.201 --> 00:09:41.301
- There was a graphic art
student at Portland State

00:09:41.301 --> 00:09:42.488
that had done some work for us,

00:09:42.488 --> 00:09:44.228
and she charged $2 an hour.

00:09:44.228 --> 00:09:46.764
And we said, how about coming up with a

00:09:46.764 --> 00:09:47.957
logo for the shoe?

00:09:47.957 --> 00:09:49.723
And she spent 17 1/2 hours on it,

00:09:49.723 --> 00:09:51.223
so it cost us $35.

00:09:52.785 --> 00:09:53.618
And as I said,

00:09:53.618 --> 00:09:54.943
it's easily worth it.

00:09:54.943 --> 00:09:57.651
It turned out to be fairly iconic.

00:09:57.651 --> 00:10:00.567
But when we went public in 1980,

00:10:00.567 --> 00:10:02.114
we gave her 500 shares of stock,

00:10:02.114 --> 00:10:03.177
which she still owns.

00:10:03.177 --> 00:10:04.580
She's never sold a share

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:06.352
and that's worth a
million dollars now too.

00:10:06.352 --> 00:10:08.396
- So good for her.

00:10:08.396 --> 00:10:09.519
- So story had a happy ending.

